Deterministic overlay labels (NOT part of the prompt — added later by the compositor):** Ƙauye, Birni, Yawan jama’a, Makaranta, Wurin lafiya, Ruwa\n\n---\n\n### 26. `p4-socs-11` — Migration and Urbanisation\n\n**Save as:** `style-b-p4-socs-11.png` → `tools/image-pipeline/output/raw/style-b-p4-socs-11.png`\n\n**Prompt:**\n\n> Clean watercolor editorial illustration for a premium children's curriculum, landscape 3:2. A reversible movement diagram between a village, town, and city with respectful family and adult-worker figures; reason icons show work, school, family care, business, land, and services, while city panels balance opportunities with needs for housing, water, transport, sanitation, health care, and schools. Translucent controlled watercolor with high-contrast focal objects and no pale or muddy washes, bright paper-white negative space, selective indigo/pencil contours around important silhouettes, clear educational visual hierarchy, culturally grounded Northern Nigerian details. Keep the upper 22% band and left/right label-safe margins completely unpainted: no wash, texture, line, icon, or incidental detail. Do not include any writing, symbols, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, anatomy-relationship lines, or circuit/wire connections in the illustration; these are added only by the separate overlay compositing step.\n\n**Avoid:**\n\n> No text, letters, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, rendered anatomy-relationship lines, rendered circuit/wire connections, watermark, logo, brand, photorealism, generic flat icon set, pale or muddy wash, dark muddy wash, low-contrast focal objects, over-rendered texture, pseudo-African patterning, caricature, unsafe child behavior, plant damage, distorted anatomy, or rendered callout labels. No trafficking, forced displacement, conflict zone, border crossing, ethnic blame, criminalisation of newcomers, poverty caricature, child moving alone, or claim that movement is always rural-to-urban or always beneficial.\n\n**Deterministic overlay labels (NOT part of the prompt — added later by the compositor):** Sauya wurin zama, Ƙauye, Birni, Aiki, Makaranta, Ƙarin hidimomi\n\n---\n\n### 27. `p4-socs-12` — Social Problems — Poverty and Community Responses\n\n**Save as:** `style-b-p4-socs-12.png` → `tools/image-pipeline/output/raw/style-b-p4-socs-12.png`\n\n**Prompt:**\n\n> Clean watercolor editorial illustration for a premium children's curriculum, landscape 3:2. A dignity-first response map connecting household needs—food, water, housing, health, clothing, and education—to public services, skills training, a mutual-aid savings group, communal work, and discreet food support; include children learning and adults in varied lawful work. Translucent controlled watercolor with high-contrast focal objects and no pale or muddy washes, bright paper-white negative space, selective indigo/pencil contours around important silhouettes, clear educational visual hierarchy, culturally grounded Northern Nigerian details. Keep the upper 22% band and left/right label-safe margins completely unpainted: no wash, texture, line, icon, or incidental detail. Do not include any writing, symbols, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, anatomy-relationship lines, or circuit/wire connections in the illustration; these are added only by the separate overlay compositing step.\n\n**Avoid:**\n\n> No text, letters, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, rendered anatomy-relationship lines, rendered circuit/wire connections, watermark, logo, brand, photorealism, generic flat icon set, pale or muddy wash, dark muddy wash, low-contrast focal objects, over-rendered texture, pseudo-African patterning, caricature, unsafe child behavior, plant damage, distorted anatomy, or rendered callout labels. No humiliating pose, torn-clothing shorthand, begging scene, named beneficiary, photography of aid, moral blame, ethnic or religious attribution, gender stereotype, political credit-claim, or guarantee that one intervention ends poverty.\n\n**Deterministic overlay labels (NOT part of the prompt — added later by the compositor):** Abinci, Ruwa, Lafiya, Ilimi, Koyar da sana’a, Taimakon juna\n\n---\n\n### 28. `p4-socs-13` — Social Problems — Child Labour and Child Rights\n\n**Save as:** `style-b-p4-socs-13.png` → `tools/image-pipeline/output/raw/style-b-p4-socs-13.png`\n\n**Prompt:**\n\n> Clean watercolor editorial illustration for a premium children's curriculum, landscape 3:2. A clear safe-versus-harmful comparison: one side shows a child briefly tidying books and toys under adult care, with school, rest, health, and play preserved; the other uses crossed-out hazard icons for heavy loads, fire, blades, chemicals, machinery, and work that blocks school, followed by a child speaking to a trusted teacher. Translucent controlled watercolor with high-contrast focal objects and no pale or muddy washes, bright paper-white negative space, selective indigo/pencil contours around important silhouettes, clear educational visual hierarchy, culturally grounded Northern Nigerian details. Keep the upper 22% band and left/right label-safe margins completely unpainted: no wash, texture, line, icon, or incidental detail. Do not include any writing, symbols, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, anatomy-relationship lines, or circuit/wire connections in the illustration; these are added only by the separate overlay compositing step.\n\n**Avoid:**\n\n> No text, letters, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, rendered anatomy-relationship lines, rendered circuit/wire connections, watermark, logo, brand, photorealism, generic flat icon set, pale or muddy wash, dark muddy wash, low-contrast focal objects, over-rendered texture, pseudo-African patterning, caricature, unsafe child behavior, plant damage, distorted anatomy, or rendered callout labels. No graphic injury, identifiable employer, real workplace accusation, forced pose, exploitative detail, child using a hazard, convention acronym or formal title, victim blame, or claim that all household help is harmful.\n\n**Deterministic overlay labels (NOT part of the prompt — added later by the compositor):** Koyo, Hutu, Wasa, Lafiya, Kariya, Amintaccen babba\n\n---\n\n### 29. `p4-socs-14` — Cooperation — Family, Community, Nation\n\n**Save as:** `style-b-p4-socs-14.png` → `tools/image-pipeline/output/raw/style-b-p4-socs-14.png`\n\n**Prompt:**\n\n> Clean watercolor editorial illustration for a premium children's curriculum, landscape 3:2. Three linked scenes: a family shares age-appropriate tasks, pupils plan a group assignment with equal speaking turns, and a Northern Nigerian community performs communal work planting trees and improving a meeting area; a small final globe panel shows countries sharing health and education knowledge. Translucent controlled watercolor with high-contrast focal objects and no pale or muddy washes, bright paper-white negative space, selective indigo/pencil contours around important silhouettes, clear educational visual hierarchy, culturally grounded Northern Nigerian details. Keep the upper 22% band and left/right label-safe margins completely unpainted: no wash, texture, line, icon, or incidental detail. Do not include any writing, symbols, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, anatomy-relationship lines, or circuit/wire connections in the illustration; these are added only by the separate overlay compositing step.\n\n**Avoid:**\n\n> No text, letters, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, rendered anatomy-relationship lines, rendered circuit/wire connections, watermark, logo, brand, photorealism, generic flat icon set, pale or muddy wash, dark muddy wash, low-contrast focal objects, over-rendered texture, pseudo-African patterning, caricature, unsafe child behavior, plant damage, distorted anatomy, or rendered callout labels. No hazardous child work, gendered domestic roles, dominant leader, coerced labor, ethnic or religious uniformity, party symbol, military alliance, international-organization logo, or suggestion that cooperation removes disagreement.\n\n**Deterministic overlay labels (NOT part of the prompt — added later by the compositor):** Haɗin kai, Gayya, Raba aiki, Sauraro, Mutunta bambanci\n\n---\n\n### 30. `p4-socs-15` — Revision and Assessment — P4 Social and Citizenship Studies\n\n**Save as:** `style-b-p4-socs-15.png` → `tools/image-pipeline/output/raw/style-b-p4-socs-15.png`\n\n**Prompt:**\n\n> Clean watercolor editorial illustration for a premium children's curriculum, landscape 3:2. A balanced revision wheel around one learner with spokes for Nigeria’s map and geography, peoples and languages, national symbols, three tiers and three arms of government, neutral civic participation, agriculture, industry and trade, population and movement, poverty responses, child rights, and cooperation. Translucent controlled watercolor with high-contrast focal objects and no pale or muddy washes, bright paper-white negative space, selective indigo/pencil contours around important silhouettes, clear educational visual hierarchy, culturally grounded Northern Nigerian details. Keep the upper 22% band and left/right label-safe margins completely unpainted: no wash, texture, line, icon, or incidental detail. Do not include any writing, symbols, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, anatomy-relationship lines, or circuit/wire connections in the illustration; these are added only by the separate overlay compositing step.\n\n**Avoid:**\n\n> No text, letters, numbers, equations, arrows, labels, rendered anatomy-relationship lines, rendered circuit/wire connections, watermark, logo, brand, photorealism, generic flat icon set, pale or muddy wash, dark muddy wash, low-contrast focal objects, over-rendered texture, pseudo-African patterning, caricature, unsafe child behavior, plant damage, distorted anatomy, or rendered callout labels. Keep all topics equal; no historical narrative, kingdom or colonial imagery, ethnic ranking, party or candidate, child voting, child labor, poverty spectacle, dangerous industry, exam score, or implication that one region represents all Nigeria.\n\n**Deterministic overlay labels (NOT part of the prompt — added later by the compositor):** Nijeriya, Jama’a, Gwamnati, Noma da kiwo, Ciniki, Yawan jama’a, Haƙƙin yara, Haɗin kai\n\n---\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
